From f4f1efb36f6963a529f07f94b3ce2411efb992bc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lucascouts <lucascs@protonmail.com>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 18:35:50 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] sidebar: drag to reorder projects and threads

The threads sidebar orders projects one step at a time, through the
header menu's Move Up and Move Down, and orders a project's threads by
activity only, so the thread someone wants on top moves whenever another
one receives a message. This patch adds three drags and a way back, the
second consumer of 0048's store after 0049: story 036, 2026-10-17.

What it does. A project header dragged onto another header moves the
project there, in this window's order (upstream's per-window
multi_workspace_state). A thread dragged inside its project group records
the order on screen and switches that project to Manual mode, where
activity no longer reorders it; the order survives a restart and shows in
every window. A pinned thread dragged inside the Pinned section reorders
the section. "Sort by Recent" in the header menu of a Manual project
returns it to Recent mode, most recently active first. Drags never cross
lists: a thread row is not accepted on a header, a group row not in the
Pinned section or another group. Drafts, terminals and the sticky header
offer no drag, and while the filter has text no thread drag is offered and
a drop records nothing, since a filtered list shows only part of the order
and recording it would erase every hidden thread's place.

How. All new code is in crates/sidebar/src/organization.rs: three typed
drag payloads (GPUI dispatches a drop by payload type, so a header ignores
a dropped thread row), pure ordering functions, and the drop and menu
handlers in an impl Sidebar block, the way pinned_section.rs reaches the
parent's private items. Project drops call upstream's
MultiWorkspace::move_project_group_up/down once per step, unchanged.
Thread and pinned drops call 0048's set_manual_order, move_pinned and
set_sort_mode; Manual rows are ordered by manual_position_in after the
final display-time sort. Every window already observes the store (0049,
Sidebar::new), so a reorder reaches the others with no new code.

Failure: a store refusal (a draft, a thread it does not know) logs one
warning naming the count or the thread id, never a title, and changes
nothing; a refused thread order also rebuilds the list from the unchanged
stores, since the rows on screen then disagree with them. A project drop
whose group left the window logs a warning without paths and moves
nothing. Without the store
(try_global is None) no drag and no menu entry are offered, as before.

Upstream touch points: crates/sidebar/src/sidebar.rs only through five
hooks, 9 hunks and 26 added lines: (1) the module lines beside
mod sidebar_pinned_tests; (2) push_entries_by_display_time takes the group
key and cx and calls organization::order_manually, with its two calls in
rebuild_contents; (3) render_project_header passes the header through
project_header_drag after .block_mouse_except_scroll(); (4) render_thread
binds its final element and returns thread_row_drag(element, ..); (5) the
header's ellipsis menu gains sort_by_recent_entry before "Remove".
crates/sidebar/src/sidebar_pinned_tests.rs: capture_logs and warn_lines
made pub(super), since a process installs its logger once. New:
crates/sidebar/src/organization.rs, sidebar_organization_tests.rs (47
tests) and sidebar_organization_filter_tests.rs (2 tests).

UNGROUPED and unconditional, right after 0049, whose Pinned section it
makes draggable and whose store observation it reuses. The
claude-agent-acp-plus patches that also edit sidebar.rs (0028-0030) and
0063 are generated on top of it; 0063 extends the filter guard to its
devtools-only view.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
